{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.manuscript_terminal_value","record":{"id":"scalar.manuscript_terminal_value","kind":"claim","label":"Terminal martingale and ground-state value formulas","status":"unverified","source_id":"scalar-r2-26-source-consequences","locator":"consequences.tex:14–73; cor:value, eq:value-identities","statement":"For an admissible minimizer and its diffusion, the bounded gradient martingale converges almost surely and in L² to U₁, with U₁²=1 almost surely. With P_* in the stated SK normalization, P_*=E|X₁|−∫₀¹t gamma(t)dt=E[U₁B₁]=∫₀¹E a(t,X_t)dt; the last integral is finite.","assumption_ids":["scalar.paper_minimizer"],"evidence_ids":[],"citation_ids":["scalar-r2-26-cite-manuscript-terminal-value","scalar_value_finite_cutoff_antecedent"],"boundary":"The terminal formula subtracts the full integral, whereas P(gamma) subtracts half. The proof uses U₁X₁=|X₁| and does not require P(X₁=0)=0 or a pointwise sign assertion at zero."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.manuscript_terminal_value","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/69473417dc487cafa5e2e39b3168d2c4a8bffdad2e91b6e486de48f122dda071","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["mapped_claim"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/targets","anchor":"targets","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/value","anchor":"value","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"short","passage_ref":"sha256:7b07c474e9e4b493474081a793473deb1d5ecae2fdcc29bf9c00dc4caa8bbe8a/papers/short.md/sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/passages/scalar-value","anchor":"scalar-value","mapping_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"expository","passage_ref":"sha256:a27286323604d97b33cbece7bb75d3d3291f7d7ec1ed0bfe12af548d92ff72dc/papers/expository.md/sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/passages/scalar-identities","anchor":"scalar-identities","mapping_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/targets","anchor":"targets","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\nWrite $u=\\Phi_x$, $a=\\Phi_{xx}$, and\n$dX_t=\\gamma(t)u(t,X_t)\\,dt+dB_t$, $X_0=0$.\nWrite $q(t)=\\mathbb E[u(t,X_t)^2]$ for the second moment of this\ngradient along $X$.\nThe first result assumes an admissible minimizer. It constructs its\nStieltjes measure and an associated diffusion, proves\n$\\operatorname{supp}\\mu_\\gamma=[0,1)$ and strict increase of $\\gamma$,\nand obtains $\\mathbb E u(t,X_t)^2=t$ and\n$\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)^2=1$ for every associated diffusion. The selected\nformal statement has exactly this conditional scope; it does not assert a\nsmooth density.\nIts [Lean implementation at the fixed source revision][support-main] implements the\n[separately stated formal challenge][support-type]. The\n[Comparator configuration][support-config] identifies the intended\ncomparison; the evidence retained with this draft does not establish\na successful research-target replay.\n\nThe second result concerns the scalar value. A bounded martingale ends\nat a spin-valued random variable whose Brownian correlation represents\n$P_*$. Finite Gaussian coefficients approximate that correlation at each\nfixed $T<1$, followed by $T\\uparrow1$. Its formal statement is separate\nand retains the minimizer and diffusion hypotheses where needed. The QAOA\ntheorem goes further: for each requested accuracy it selects one finite\ndeterministic circuit, independent of size and disorder, whose limiting\nexpected energy approaches $P_*$. Neither formal statement asserts that\ncircuit conclusion.\nThe scalar clauses are in a [separate Lean implementation][value-main], with their\nown [formal challenge][value-type] and [Comparator configuration][value-config].\nThose links identify the selected statements, not a completed replay\nreceipt for either research target.\n","span":{"start_byte":5235,"end_byte":6976,"start_line":103,"end_line":133},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=targets","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=targets"},{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/value","anchor":"value","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n**Proposition 6.1 (terminal value).** For the minimizer and diffusion of\nTheorem 5.1 there is a spin-valued terminal variable $U_1$ such that\n$u(t,X_t)=\\mathbb E[U_1\\mid\\mathcal F_t]$ and\n$$\nP_*=\\mathbb E[U_1B_1]=\\int_0^1\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)\\,dt.\n\\tag{6.1}\n$$\nIts finite-cutoff tail lies in $[0,1-T]$. This proposition uses the\nattained Parisi formula in addition to the support theorem.\n\nThe bounded martingale $u(t,X_t)$ converges almost surely and in $L^2$\nto $U_1$. Its second moment tends to one because $q(t)=t$.\nSince $|U_1|\\le1$, $\\mathbb E U_1^2=1$ forces $U_1^2=1$ almost surely.\nUniform integrability identifies $u(t,X_t)=\\mathbb E[U_1\\mid\\mathcal F_t]$,\nwhich is the conditional-expectation identity used below.\n\nThe drift bound $|\\gamma u|\\le\\gamma\\in L^1$ gives convergence of $X_t$\nto $X_1$. Uniform terminal convergence of $\\Phi$ to $|x|$, together\nwith convexity, gives $u(t,x_t)\\to\\operatorname{sgn}(x)$ when\n$x_t\\to x\\ne0$. Thus $U_1X_1=|X_1|$, including when $X_1=0$;\nno proof that this event has probability zero is needed.\n\nItô's formula gives\n$$\n\\mathbb E\\Phi(t,X_t)=\\Phi(0,0)+\\frac12\\int_0^t\\gamma(s)s\\,ds.\n$$\nThe terminal limit first gives\n$\\Phi(0,0)=\\mathbb E|X_1|-\\tfrac12\\int_0^1s\\gamma(s)ds$.\nSubtracting the second half from the definition of the functional yields\n$\\mathcal P(\\gamma)=\\mathbb E|X_1|-\\int_0^1s\\gamma(s)\\,ds$.\nThe ground-state Parisi formula identifies this with $P_*$.\nThe diffusion equation, conditional expectation and Fubini give\n$$\n\\mathbb E[U_1X_1]=\\mathbb E[U_1B_1]\n +\\int_0^1\\gamma(s)\\mathbb E[u(s,X_s)^2]\\,ds.\n$$\nBounded $u,U_1$ and integrable $\\gamma$ justify these terms.\nCombining the identities and using stochastic integral covariance gives\n$$\nP_*=\\mathbb E|X_1|-\\int_0^1t\\gamma(t)\\,dt\n =\\mathbb E[U_1B_1]=\\int_0^1\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)\\,dt.\n$$\nThe last integral is absolutely bounded by one because\n$\\mathbb E|a|\\le(\\mathbb E a^2)^{1/2}=1$.\nFull support supplies $\\mathbb E a^2=1$, whereas the value uses\n$\\int\\mathbb E a$. Substituting the second moment into the value identity\nwould change the assertion. [OpenAI (2026a), §7][fs-cons].\nThe prediction error and the energy tail have different sizes:\n$$\n\\|U_1-u(T,X_T)\\|_2^2=1-T,\n$$\n$$\nP_*-\\int_0^T\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)\\,dt\n=\\mathbb E[(U_1-u(T,X_T))(B_1-B_T)]\\in[0,1-T].\n$$\nThe first identity is orthogonal projection onto $\\mathcal F_T$.\nMartingale orthogonality removes the past Brownian component in the\nsecond; Cauchy–Schwarz bounds the product by $1-T$, and convexity\nmakes it nonnegative. No uniform terminal curvature bound is needed.\nThe stochastic value calculation has a direct antecedent in\n[Sellke, arXiv:2105.03506v4, Definition 1.3 and Lemma 3.11](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.03506v4).\nThere, “optimizable” means the contact identity $\\mathbb E u(t,X_t)^2=t$\non $[q,1)$, with $q$ the infimum of the support of the coefficient\nfunction. Lemma 3.11 derives the finite-cutoff value identity by applying\nItô's formula to $\\Phi$ and $xu$, before taking the terminal limit.\nThis is the same value-recovery mechanism used here, specialized to\nzero field and $\\xi''=1$.\n\nA cutoff must retain the full Parisi penalty. Set\n$\\Psi(T,x)=\\Phi(T,x)-xu(T,x)$ and\n$I(T)=\\int_0^T\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)\\,dt$. Subtracting the complete penalty\nfrom the finite-time Itô identity gives\n$$\n\\mathcal P(\\gamma)-I(T)\n =\\mathbb E\\Psi(T,X_T)\n  +\\frac12\\int_0^T\\gamma(t)(q(t)-t)\\,dt\n  -\\frac12\\int_T^1t\\gamma(t)\\,dt.\n$$\nUnder contact the middle term vanishes. The final term is absent from\nthe displayed equality in [El Alaoui, Montanari and Sellke,\narXiv:2001.00904v1, §6.2, pp. 32–33][eams20]. Its sign is nonpositive,\nso the upper bound by $\\mathbb E\\Psi(T,X_T)$ used there remains valid;\nits magnitude tends to zero by integrability. This finite-cutoff\ncorrection is consistent with the exact terminal identity above.\n","span":{"start_byte":77502,"end_byte":81318,"start_line":1729,"end_line":1813},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=value","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=value"},{"stage":"short","passage_ref":"sha256:7b07c474e9e4b493474081a793473deb1d5ecae2fdcc29bf9c00dc4caa8bbe8a/papers/short.md/sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/passages/scalar-value","anchor":"scalar-value","mapping_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n### 5.1 The scalar value\n\nThe support identities give a scalar representation of $P_*$. Set\n$M_t=u(t,X_t)$, the bounded gradient martingale satisfying\n$dM_t=a(t,X_t)\\,dB_t$.\nThe quadratic variations distinguish the two Hessian moments:\n$$\nd\\langle M\\rangle_t=a(t,X_t)^2\\,dt,\n\\qquad d\\langle M,B\\rangle_t=a(t,X_t)\\,dt.\n$$\nFull support gives $\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)^2=1$, hence each increment\n$M_t-M_s$ has unconditional variance $t-s$. This normalises the\ndiscrete increments below. The first moment of $a$ instead gives\ncovariance with the driving noise. Let $U$ be the almost-sure and\n$L^2$ limit of $M_t$. The value identity is\n$$\nP_*=\\mathbb E[UB_1]\n   =\\int_0^1\\mathbb E[a(t,X_t)]\\,dt.                \\tag{10}\n$$\nBoundedness gives\n$|U|\\leq1$, while $\\mathbb E M_t^2\\to1$ gives $\\mathbb E U^2=1$;\ntherefore $U^2=1$ almost surely. To see the cancellation behind (10), the\nterminal convergence of the convex value functions gives $UX_1=|X_1|$, while Itô's formula gives\n$\\mathcal P(\\gamma)=\\mathbb E|X_1|-\\int_0^1t\\gamma(t)\\,dt$.\nMultiplying $X_1=B_1+\\int_0^1\\gamma(t)M_t\\,dt$ by $U$ cancels\nthat integral because $\\mathbb E[UM_t]=t$. The Parisi formula then\ngives (10). The endpoint limits and stochastic-integral covariance\nare proved in [OpenAI (2026a), Corollary 7.1][FS] and the long\nrecord, <span class=\"pdf-companion\" data-pdf-target=\"../long/reasoning-record.pdf\" data-pdf-anchor=\"scalar-value-identities\">[§6, “Recovering the value from a martingale”](../long/reasoning-record.md#scalar-value-identities)</span>. This argument\nuses full support, not the density corollary. In particular, convexity\nand Cauchy–Schwarz give $0\\leq\\mathbb E a\\leq1$, so\n$$\n0\\leq P_*-\\int_0^T\\mathbb E[a(t,X_t)]\\,dt\\leq1-T.\n$$\nOne can therefore fix the terminal cutoff before discretising the diffusion.\n\nThe gradient-martingale and value methods have antecedents in\n[Montanari, preprint v2, Lemmas 3.2–3.4][Montanari], under its\npositive-temperature support assumption, and in the zero-temperature\ncalculations of [El Alaoui, Montanari and Sellke, preprint v1,\nLemmas 6.5 and 6.13][EAMS]. [Sellke, preprint v4,\nProposition 3.10 and Lemma 3.11, p. 17][Sellke] gives the closely related\nfinite-cutoff cancellation by applying Itô's formula to $\\Phi$ and $x\\Phi_x$.\n\n### 5.2 The classical algorithm\n\nFull support and existence give an attained, strictly increasing\nminimiser in the monotone zero-temperature class. This is Assumption 2 of\n[El Alaoui, Montanari and Sellke, preprint v1][EAMS]. Their Corollary 2.2\nthen gives, for every fixed $\\varepsilon>0$, a randomised algorithm\nreturning a configuration within $\\varepsilon n$ of the finite SK\nmaximum with probability tending to one over couplings and algorithmic\nrandomness. Its cost is $C(\\varepsilon)n^2$ real-arithmetic operations\nwith the prescribed scalar functions and normalisers available; this\nincludes no separate numerical-preprocessing or finite-precision bound.\n[OpenAI (2026a), Corollary 7.3][FS] and <span class=\"pdf-companion\" data-pdf-target=\"../long/reasoning-record.pdf\" data-pdf-anchor=\"classical-algorithm-consequence\">[long §9.4](../long/reasoning-record.md#classical-algorithm-consequence)</span>\ncheck the Gaussian convention and operation count. Chen already records\nthe classical consequence in [§1, p. 4][Chen].\nIn their incremental approximate message-passing construction, the\nnoise and drift coefficients are, in our notation,\n$$\nu_{\\rm EAMS}(t,x)=a(t,x),\\qquad\nv_{\\rm EAMS}(t,x)=\\gamma(t)u(t,x).\n$$\nThus their noise multiplier is the Hessian, whereas our $u$ is the\ngradient. The bounded martingale $M_t$ and $\\mathbb E a^2=1$ give\nthe required variance normalisation. With the fixed-strip regularity\nand discretisation estimates, [their Theorem 2][EAMS] achieves energy\narbitrarily close to the truncated first-moment integral in (10).\nStrict positivity of the density is unnecessary.\n\n","span":{"start_byte":32328,"end_byte":36198,"start_line":680,"end_line":751},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/short?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=short&passage=scalar-value","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=short&passage=scalar-value"},{"stage":"expository","passage_ref":"sha256:a27286323604d97b33cbece7bb75d3d3291f7d7ec1ed0bfe12af548d92ff72dc/papers/expository.md/sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70d